use std
into ole.amni

This file is a module with no main (namespace ole). It reads legacy Microsoft Office binary files into the document model of module `dm` (blocks, metadata, warnings): Word 97 to 2003 (.doc), Excel 97 to 2003 (.xls), PowerPoint 97 to 2003 (.ppt) and Outlook messages (.msg). They are compound files, opened with the reader of module `cfb`. `ol_read` takes the whole file's bytes, returns false when the file is none of these or cannot be read (the model may then hold nothing but warnings), and expects the caller to have called `dm::dm_reset` already. Spreadsheet numbers and dates are written the way module `office` writes them for XLSX, so it supplies `office::of_fmt_is_date`, `office::of_builtin_date`, `office::of_serial_text` and `office::of_pad`. Every module-level name starts with `ol_` or `OL_`.

```amni
import dm;
import cfb;
import office;
import html;
import rtf;
import textfmt;
import binary;
```

Conventions used below: "read a 16-bit (or 32-bit) number at p of s" means `cfb::cf_le(s, p, 2)` (or 4), which is little endian and gives -1 when p is out of range; callers treat -1 as a failure. "The stream NAME" means the stream of the open compound file with that path, read with `cfb::cf_get(ol_h, NAME, list)`. A scratch list is a local `byte[>..]` created empty. Module state shared by every reader: `var ol_h = -1` (the handle of the open compound file), `var ol_enc = false` (set when an encrypted file is met).

## Shared helpers

`ol_sx(v int, bits int) int` is v read as a signed number of that many bits: v minus 2 to the power bits when v is at least 2 to the power bits minus 1, otherwise v.

`ol_cp(cp int, out byte[>..]&)` appends the code point cp to out as UTF-8 with `dm::dm_put_cp`.

`ol_trim0(s const byte[:]) const byte[:]` is s without the zero bytes at its end.

`ol_flat(s byte[>..]&)` replaces every tab, newline and carriage return byte of s with a space, in place.

`ol_filetime(ft int, out byte[>..]&)` appends a Windows FILETIME (100 nanosecond ticks since 1601-01-01) as text like "2026-03-01T10:00:00Z": compute the spreadsheet serial number as the real ft divided by 864000000000.0 minus 109205.0 and call `office::of_serial_text(serial, false, text)` on a scratch list; when that returns false append nothing; otherwise replace the first space in the text by "T" (when it has no space append "T00:00:00" instead) and append the text and "Z" to out.

## Document properties

The property set stream "\u0005SummaryInformation" is the byte 5 followed by the letters SummaryInformation. A property set stream has a header of 28 bytes whose last 4 bytes (at offset 24) count the property sets, then the 16-byte format id and a 4-byte offset (at 44) of the first set. A set starts with its size (4 bytes) and its property count (4 bytes), then for each property a pair of id (4 bytes) and offset (4 bytes) counted from the start of the set; the value there starts with a type (2 bytes) and 2 bytes of padding.

`ol_prop(s const byte[:], id int, out byte[>..]&) bool` finds property id in the first property set of s and appends its value as text to out. It returns false when s is too short, the set or property does not exist, or the type is not handled. Types: 0x1E (an 8-bit string: length at value plus 4 including its zero byte, bytes from value plus 8) goes through `dm::dm_to_utf8` with the trailing zero bytes removed (`ol_trim0`); 0x1F (UTF-16: the character count at value plus 4 including the zero, the text from value plus 8) goes through `dm::dm_utf16(bytes, false, out)` (its second parameter is `big`, so false reads little endian) with the trailing zero characters removed; 0x40 (a FILETIME, 8 bytes at value plus 4, read as two 32-bit halves, high times 4294967296 plus low) goes through `ol_filetime`; 0x03 (a 32-bit signed number at value plus 4) appends its decimal text.

`ol_meta(pages bool)` reads the stream "\u0005SummaryInformation" (byte 5 then SummaryInformation; nothing happens when it is missing) and calls `dm::dm_set_meta` in this order with the text of `ol_prop`: "title" from property 2, "subject" 3, "author" 4, "keywords" 5, "created" 12, "modified" 13, and, when pages is true, "pages" from property 14.

## Word 97 to 2003

Module state: `var ol_wd byte[>..]` (the WordDocument stream), `var ol_tb byte[>..]` (the table stream), `var ol_w byte[>..]` (every character of the document as UTF-16 little endian, two bytes each, in character position order), `var ol_pcp int[>..]` (the starting character position of each piece followed by the end of the last one), `var ol_pfc int[>..]` (each piece's byte offset in `ol_wd`), `var ol_pcm byte[>..]` (1 when the piece is 8-bit text), `var ol_ph = 0` (the piece found last), `var ol_slv int[>..]` (the heading level of each style number, 0 for none), run lists describing paragraph properties: `var ol_rfc int[>..]` and `var ol_rfe int[>..]` (the start and end file offsets of a run), `var ol_ris int[>..]` (style number), `var ol_rfl int[>..]` (bit 1 for in a table, bit 2 for end of a table row), `var ol_ril int[>..]` (list number), `var ol_rlv int[>..]` (list level) and `var ol_rol int[>..]` (outline level, 9 when none); field state: `var ol_fst int[>..]` (a stack: 1 for a field still in its code, 2 once in its result) and `var ol_fcode = 0` (how many fields are in code state); table state: `var ol_tbo = false` (a table is open), `var ol_rowb byte[>..]`, `var ol_cellb byte[>..]` and `var ol_cols = 0` (the cells in the row so far); `var ol_au byte[>..]`, `var ol_aat int[>..]` and `var ol_alen int[>..]` (comment author names, the text and its positions).

`ol_wd_pair(n int, fc int&, lcb int&) bool` reads entry number n of the FIB's table of file offset and length pairs: it is false when n is not below the 16-bit number at 0x98 of `ol_wd` (the pair count) or when the length is 0 or not found; otherwise it sets fc from the 32-bit number at 0x9A plus 8 times n and lcb from the one at 0x9E plus 8 times n and returns true.

`ol_wd_pieces() bool` reads the piece table. Entry 33 of the pairs gives the Clx in `ol_tb`. Clear `ol_pcp`, `ol_pfc` and `ol_pcm`. Start p at fc of the Clx and repeat: the byte at p is 1 (a property list: skip 3 plus the 16-bit number at p plus 1 bytes) or 2 (the piece table, stop); anything else makes the function return false. For the 2: the byte count lcb2 is the 32-bit number at p plus 1 and the piece table (PlcPcd) starts at p plus 5; the piece count n is (lcb2 minus 4) divided by 12 (false when n is below 1 or above 1000000). For piece i the character position is the 32-bit number at the table's start plus 4 times i (push all n plus 1 of them to `ol_pcp`), and its descriptor is 8 bytes at the table's start plus 4 times (n plus 1) plus 8 times i: take the 32-bit number at descriptor offset 2 as raw; bit 30 of raw (0x40000000) marks 8-bit text; the offset is raw with bits above 29 cleared, divided by 2 for 8-bit text; push the offset to `ol_pfc` and the mark (1 or 0) to `ol_pcm`. Return true.

`ol_wd_text()` clears `ol_w` and appends the text of every piece in order: a piece holds its end character position minus its start characters; for an 8-bit piece each byte b at the piece's offset in `ol_wd` becomes the code unit `dm::dm_cp1252(b)` written as two little-endian bytes; for a UTF-16 piece the bytes are copied as they are. A piece whose bytes extend past the end of `ol_wd` is cut to what is there and `dm::dm_warn_add` gets "document text is truncated". Stop adding once `ol_w` holds 40000000 bytes.

`ol_wd_unit(i int) int` is the 16-bit code unit number i of `ol_w` (-1 when i is out of range).

`ol_wd_cp2fc(cp int) int` is the file offset in `ol_wd` of the character at position cp: find the piece whose positions hold cp (start looking at `ol_ph`, going back to 0 when cp is below that piece's start, and remember the piece in `ol_ph`), and return its offset plus cp minus its start (times 2 for a UTF-16 piece); -1 when no piece holds cp.

`ol_wd_styles()` reads the style sheet into `ol_slv`. Clear `ol_slv`. Entry 1 of the pairs gives its offset st and length in `ol_tb`; do nothing when `ol_wd_pair` is false. The first 16-bit number at st is the size cbs of the header; the style count cstd is the 16-bit number at st plus 2 and the base size cb0 is the 16-bit number at st plus 4. Styles follow from st plus 2 plus cbs: for each of the cstd styles (at most 4000) read the size sz (16 bits) at p, move p to p plus 2, and when sz is at least cb0 plus 2: the style's identity sti is the 16-bit number at p with bits above 11 cleared, and its name is the UTF-16 text after the 16-bit character count at p plus cb0 (converted with `dm::dm_utf16(bytes, false, out)`, where false means little endian). The level pushed to `ol_slv` is: 1 to 9 for sti 1 to 9 limited to at most 6, 1 for sti 62 (the Title style), otherwise the level taken from the name compared ignoring case ("heading" then a space and a digit 1 to 9, limited to 6, or exactly "title" for 1), otherwise 0. A style with sz below cb0 plus 2 pushes 0. Then p moves to p plus sz.

`ol_wd_sprms(p int, end int, fl int&, ilfo int&, ilvl int&, ol int&)` reads the property modifiers in `ol_wd` from p up to end. Each starts with a 16-bit opcode; the operand size depends on the top 3 bits of the opcode: 0 and 1 give 1 byte, 2, 4 and 5 give 2 bytes, 3 gives 4 bytes, 7 gives 3 bytes and 6 gives 1 plus the value of the byte after the opcode; the operand starts at p plus 2 and the next modifier at p plus 2 plus the size. Stop at end. By opcode: 0x2416 sets bit 1 of fl when the byte is not 0, 0x2417 sets bit 2 of fl when the byte is not 0, 0x460B sets ilfo to the 16-bit operand, 0x260A sets ilvl to the byte and 0x2640 sets ol to the byte.

`ol_wd_fkp(pg int)` appends the paragraph runs of the 512-byte page at offset pg of `ol_wd` (nothing when it does not fit): the run count crun is the last byte of the page; run i spans the file offsets given by the 32-bit numbers i and i plus 1 at the start of the page; its descriptor byte is the byte at pg plus 4 times (crun plus 1) plus 13 times i, and when that byte b is not 0 the properties are at pg plus 2 times b: the byte there is cb; when cb is 0 the next byte cb2 gives size 2 times cb2 and the data starts 2 bytes on; otherwise the size is 2 times cb minus 1 and the data starts 1 byte on; when the size is at least 2 the style number is the 16-bit number at the data's start and `ol_wd_sprms` reads the modifiers from the data's start plus 2 up to its start plus the size. Starting values are fl 0, ilfo 0, ilvl 0, ol 9 and style 0. Push the run's start and end offsets, style, fl, ilfo, ilvl and ol to `ol_rfc`, `ol_rfe`, `ol_ris`, `ol_rfl`, `ol_ril`, `ol_rlv` and `ol_rol`.

`ol_wd_papx()` clears the run lists, then, when `ol_wd_pair(13, ...)` gives the offset and length of the paragraph bin table in `ol_tb`, reads its count n as (length minus 4) divided by 8 (at most 100000) and calls `ol_wd_fkp` with 512 times the low 22 bits of the 32-bit number at offset plus 4 times (n plus 1) plus 4 times k for each k below n.

`ol_wd_run(fc int) int` is the index of the run that holds the file offset fc: binary search `ol_rfc` for the last start not above fc and require fc below that run's end in `ol_rfe`; -1 when there is none.

`ol_wd_put(u int, pb byte[>..]&)` handles one character unit u of the text: 0x13 pushes 1 on `ol_fst` (ignored when it already has 70 entries) and adds 1 to `ol_fcode`; 0x14 changes the top of `ol_fst` from 1 to 2 and takes 1 off `ol_fcode` (nothing when the top is not 1); 0x15 pops `ol_fst` and takes 1 off `ol_fcode` when the popped value was 1. Otherwise, when `ol_fcode` is above 0 the unit is dropped (a field code); else 9 appends a tab, 0x0B a newline, 0x0C a space, 0x1E a hyphen, and 0x1F and every other unit below 32 append nothing; any other unit is appended to pb as two little-endian bytes.

`ol_wd_flat(c0 int, c1 int, out byte[>..]&)` appends to out as UTF-8 the text of the characters from position c0 up to but not including c1 with `ol_wd_put` (a scratch UTF-16 list converted with `dm::dm_utf16(bytes, false, out)`, where false means little endian), except that the units 0x0D and 0x07 become a newline instead, and the result is trimmed of white space at both ends in out (only what this call appended). Start with `ol_fst` cleared and `ol_fcode` 0.

`ol_wd_close_table()` does nothing when `ol_tbo` is false; otherwise, when `ol_rowb` is not empty add it as `dm::DM_ROW` (level 0) and clear it, then call `dm::dm_end_table()`, and set `ol_tbo` false, `ol_cols` 0 and clear `ol_cellb`.

`ol_wd_emit(txt byte[>..]&, cp int, term int)` adds one finished paragraph whose UTF-8 text is txt, whose first character is at position cp and whose last unit was term (0x0D or 0x07). Find its run with `ol_wd_run(ol_wd_cp2fc(cp))` (the flags are 0 when there is none). When `ol_rfc` is empty (no paragraph properties at all) a term of 0x07 counts as in a table, and an empty text with term 0x07 right after a cell counts as the end of a row. In a table: when `ol_tbo` is false call `dm::dm_new_table()` and set it true; when the flags have bit 2 it is the end of a row: add `ol_rowb` as `dm::DM_ROW` if not empty, clear `ol_rowb` and `ol_cols`; otherwise, when term is 0x07 it ends a cell: take txt (with `ol_flat`) joined to `ol_cellb` by a space when both are not empty, append a tab to `ol_rowb` when `ol_cols` is above 0, append the cell, add 1 to `ol_cols` and clear `ol_cellb`; otherwise (a paragraph inside a cell) append txt to `ol_cellb` with a space between when both are not empty. Outside a table: call `ol_wd_close_table()`, then the heading level is the run's outline level plus 1 (at most 6) when that outline level is 0 to 8, else `ol_slv` of the run's style when it is in range, else 0; add `dm::DM_HEAD` with that level when it is above 0; otherwise a list item `dm::DM_ITEM` with level ilvl plus 1 when the run's list number is 1 to 2047; otherwise `dm::DM_PARA` with level 0; all through `dm::dm_add`.

`ol_wd_body(c0 int, c1 int)` turns the characters from position c0 up to but not including c1 into blocks: clear `ol_fst`, set `ol_fcode` 0, keep a scratch UTF-16 list pb and the paragraph's first position p0; for each position i the unit is `ol_wd_unit(i)`: when it is 0x0D or 0x07 convert pb to UTF-8 with `dm::dm_utf16(pb, false, text)` (the second parameter is `big`, false means little endian) and call `ol_wd_emit(text, p0, unit)`, clear pb and set p0 to i plus 1; any other unit goes through `ol_wd_put(unit, pb)`. At the end, when pb is not empty (text with no final paragraph mark) emit it the same way with term 0x0D; then call `ol_wd_close_table()`.

`ol_wd_authors()` clears `ol_au`, `ol_aat` and `ol_alen` and, when `ol_wd_pair(36, ...)` gives the offset and length of the comment author list in `ol_tb`, reads names one after another until the end: a 16-bit character count then that many UTF-16 characters (converted with `dm::dm_utf16(bytes, false, out)`, where false means little endian), each appended to `ol_au` with its position and length pushed.

`ol_wd_notes(kind int)` adds the notes of one kind, 1 footnotes, 2 endnotes, 3 comments. Let ccp be the 32-bit numbers at 0x4C (text), 0x50 (footnotes), 0x54 (headers), 0x58 (macros), 0x5C (comments), 0x60 (endnotes). The story starts are: footnotes at text; comments at text plus footnotes plus headers plus macros; endnotes at the comment start plus comments. The table of text positions is entry 3 of the pairs for footnotes, 47 for endnotes and 5 for comments (do nothing when `ol_wd_pair` is false or the story has no characters); its count n is its length divided by 4 minus 1 and its numbers are positions relative to the story start. For comments call `ol_wd_authors()` first and also read entry 4 of the pairs (the comment reference table: its own record count nr is its length minus 4, divided by 34, and its records of 30 bytes start at its offset plus 4 times (nr plus 1); the 16-bit number at offset 20 of record i is the author number, which is only read when i is below nr, so the text table count n and nr are separate numbers). For note number i from 0 below n, text is `ol_wd_flat` of the story start plus position i up to the story start plus position i plus 1; skip it when it is blank. Add `dm::dm_add(dm::DM_NOTE, 0, line)` where the line is for a comment "Comment by ", the author, ": " when the author name exists and is not empty, otherwise "Comment: "; for the others "Footnote " or "Endnote ", the number i plus 1, ": "; followed by the text.

`ol_doc() bool` reads a Word document from the open compound file. Read the stream "WordDocument" into `ol_wd` (false when missing). Require the 16-bit number at 0 to be 0xA5EC, else false. When the 16-bit number at 2 (nFib) is below 193 call `dm::dm_warn_add("Word 6 or 95 document: not supported")` and return false. When bit 8 (0x100) of the 16-bit number at 10 is set call `dm::dm_warn_add("encrypted Word document: it needs its password")`, set `ol_enc` and return false. Read the table stream, "1Table" when bit 9 (0x200) of that number is set and "0Table" otherwise, into `ol_tb` (false when it cannot be read). Clear the field state and table state. Then call `ol_wd_pieces()` (false when it fails) and `ol_wd_text()`, `ol_wd_styles()`, `ol_wd_papx()`. The main text is the characters from 0 up to the 32-bit number at 0x4C; call `ol_wd_body(0, that)`. Then the text boxes: they start at text plus footnotes plus headers plus macros plus comments plus endnotes (the numbers at 0x4C, 0x50, 0x54, 0x58, 0x5C, 0x60) and last for the number at 0x64; call `ol_wd_body` on them when that is above 0. Then `ol_wd_notes(1)`, `ol_wd_notes(2)` and `ol_wd_notes(3)`. Then `ol_meta(true)`. Return true.


## Excel 97 to 2003

The workbook is the stream "Workbook" (BIFF8) or "Book" (BIFF5), a sequence of records: a 16-bit id, a 16-bit data length n, then n bytes. Module state: `var ol_xb byte[>..]` (the stream), `var ol_b5 = false` (true for BIFF5, whose strings are 8-bit), `var ol_x1904 = false`, `var ol_xs byte[>..]` and `var ol_xse int[>..]` (the shared string table bytes and the end offset of each record it was joined from), `var ol_sst byte[>..]`, `var ol_ssa int[>..]` and `var ol_ssl int[>..]` (shared strings as UTF-8 with their positions and lengths), `var ol_xfi int[>..]` (the number format id of each XF record), `var ol_fid int[>..]` and `var ol_fdt byte[>..]` (custom format ids and 1 when the format shows dates), `var ol_shp int[>..]` (the stream offset of each worksheet), `var ol_shn byte[>..]`, `var ol_sha int[>..]` and `var ol_shl int[>..]` (worksheet names as UTF-8 with positions and lengths), the cells of the sheet being read `var ol_cr int[>..]`, `var ol_cc int[>..]` (row and column), `var ol_ca int[>..]` and `var ol_cl int[>..]` (position and length of the cell text in `var ol_xp byte[>..]`), `var ol_fpend = -1` (the formula cell waiting for its string), `var ol_cnt int[>..]` and `var ol_ord int[>..]` (scratch for ordering the cells).

`ol_hex(out byte[>..]&, v int, width int)` appends the upper-case hexadecimal digits of the non-negative int v to out, padded with leading zeros to at least width digits.

`ol_xl_str(s const byte[:], p int, short bool, out byte[>..]&) int` reads a BIFF string at p of s and appends its text as UTF-8 to out; it returns the position after the string, or -1 when it does not fit. short true means a 1-byte character count, false a 2-byte one. In BIFF5 (`ol_b5` true) the count is followed directly by that many 8-bit characters, each added with `dm::dm_put_cp(out, dm::dm_cp1252(byte))`. Otherwise a flags byte follows the count: bit 0 means 16-bit characters (little endian code units, kept as units and converted with `dm::dm_utf16(units, false, out)` where false means little endian), else 8-bit characters, each a code point equal to the byte; bit 3 means a 16-bit count of formatting runs follows the flags; bit 2 means a 32-bit length of phonetic data follows after that. The characters come next; after them skip 4 bytes per formatting run and the phonetic length.

`ol_xl_sstr(pos int&) bool` reads one shared string from `ol_xs` at pos: the 16-bit character count, the flags byte, then as in `ol_xl_str` the run count (flag bit 3) and the phonetic length (flag bit 2). Then the characters, one at a time, with the rule for continued records: keep an index seg into `ol_xse`; before each character, while seg is not the last index and pos is at or past `ol_xse[seg]`, add 1 to seg, and when pos equals the end of the segment just left (`ol_xse[seg minus 1]`) the next byte is a new flags byte: read it, take bit 0 as the new 16-bit flag for the rest of the string and add 1 to pos. A character is one byte (8-bit) or two bytes (16-bit) at pos; 8-bit characters are code points equal to the byte; add each with `dm::dm_put_cp` to a scratch list. After the characters skip 4 bytes per run and the phonetic length. Append the scratch text to `ol_sst` and push its position and length to `ol_ssa` and `ol_ssl`. It returns false when the data runs out before the string ends (the string is then still pushed with what was read).

`ol_xl_sst(p int) int` handles the shared string table record whose header is at p of `ol_xb` and returns the position after its last continuation. Clear `ol_xs` and `ol_xse`, `ol_sst`, `ol_ssa` and `ol_ssl`. Append the data of the record to `ol_xs` and push its length to `ol_xse`; then, while the record after the last one read has id 0x003C (a continuation), append its data too and push the running total length of `ol_xs` to `ol_xse`. The 32-bit number at offset 4 is the unique string count (stop reading at 1000000); starting at pos 8 call `ol_xl_sstr(pos)` that many times, stopping when it returns false or pos reaches the end of `ol_xs`.

`ol_xl_fmt(ixfe int) int` is the kind of number format of an XF record: 0 for a plain number, 1 for a date format, 2 for a boolean format. It is 0 when ixfe is not a valid index into `ol_xfi`. When the format id is in `ol_fid` it is the matching entry of `ol_fdt`; otherwise it is 1 when `office::of_builtin_date` of the id is true and 0 when not.

`ol_xl_isdate(ixfe int) bool` is true when `ol_xl_fmt(ixfe)` is 1.

`ol_xl_num(v real, out byte[>..]&)` appends a number the way `office::of_num_text` does: when the absolute value is below 1e15 and v equals `ifloor(v)` append `format(out, ifloor(v))` (an integer, never "10.0"), otherwise append `pyg(v, 15)` (fifteen significant digits, trailing zeros removed).

`ol_xl_rk(rk int) real` decodes an RK number: when bit 1 of rk is set the value is the 30-bit signed integer in bits 2 to 31 (rk shifted right by 2, minus 1073741824 when that is 536870912 or more); otherwise build an 8-byte list holding four zero bytes and then the 4 bytes of rk with the two lowest bits cleared (little endian) and read it with `binary::read_f64le(list, 0)`, which returns the real and a bool. When bit 0 of rk is set divide the value by 100.0.

`ol_xl_err(code int, out byte[>..]&)` appends the error name for a BIFF error code: 0 "#NULL!", 7 "#DIV/0!", 15 "#VALUE!", 23 "#REF!", 29 "#NAME?", 36 "#NUM!", 42 "#N/A"; any other code appends nothing.

`ol_xl_put(row int, col int, txt const byte[:]) int` records one cell and returns its index in the cell lists, or -1 when row is not in 0 to 65535 or col not in 0 to 255 (nothing is recorded then): push row and col, the position (the current length of `ol_xp`) and the length of txt, and append txt to `ol_xp`.

`ol_xl_numcell(row int, col int, ixfe int, v real)` records a numeric cell through `ol_xl_put`: when `ol_xl_fmt(ixfe)` is 2 record "TRUE" when v is not 0.0 and "FALSE" when it is; otherwise, when `ol_xl_isdate(ixfe)` and `office::of_serial_text(v, ol_x1904, text)` returns true (text is a scratch list) record the date text, otherwise record `ol_xl_num` of v.

`ol_xl_globals() int` reads the workbook-level records and returns the number of worksheets found, or -1 when this is not a usable workbook and -2 when it is encrypted. The first record must have id 0x0809 (BOF) with a 16-bit version at data offset 0 and a 16-bit type at offset 2 equal to 5 (workbook globals), else return -1; set `ol_b5` true when the version is below 0x0600. Clear `ol_x1904`, `ol_xfi`, `ol_fid`, `ol_fdt`, `ol_shp`, `ol_shn`, `ol_sha`, `ol_shl`, `ol_sst`, `ol_ssa` and `ol_ssl`. Go through the records from the start (stop at the end of the stream, at a record that does not fit, or at id 0x000A, the end of the globals), by id: 0x002F (FILEPASS): call `dm::dm_warn_add("encrypted Excel workbook: it needs its password")`, set `ol_enc` true and return -2; 0x0022: `ol_x1904` is true when the 16-bit number at data offset 0 is 1; 0x0085 (a sheet): the 32-bit number at offset 0 is the stream offset of the sheet and the byte at offset 5 its type, 0 for a worksheet (any other type is ignored); its name is `ol_xl_str(s, 6, true, name)` taken on the record's data (for BIFF8 and BIFF5) with the data as s; for a worksheet push the offset to `ol_shp` and the name to `ol_shn`, `ol_sha` and `ol_shl`; 0x041E (a number format): the 16-bit number at offset 0 is the id and the format code is `ol_xl_str` at offset 2 (a 2-byte count for BIFF8, in BIFF5 a 1-byte count so use short true); push the id to `ol_fid` and to `ol_fdt` 2 when the code lower-cased (ASCII) is exactly the text "TRUE";"TRUE";"FALSE" with its double quotes and semicolons (a boolean format), else 1 when `office::of_fmt_is_date` of the code is true, else 0; 0x00E0 (an XF record): push the 16-bit number at data offset 2 to `ol_xfi`; 0x00FC: `ol_xl_sst(p)` gives the position to continue at. Return the number of worksheets.

`ol_xl_rows(name const byte[:])` turns the cells in the lists into table rows. Do nothing when there are no cells. Fill `ol_cnt` with 65537 zeros and add 1 to entry row plus 1 for every cell; turn it into running totals (entry r plus 1 gets entry r added); then fill `ol_ord` with the cell indexes grouped by row in their original order (a stable counting sort: copy the starts, place each cell index at its row's next slot). Then for each row from 0 to 65535 that has cells: sort its slice of `ol_ord` by column with a stable insertion sort (move an index left while the column before it is greater); add 1 to the count of rows, and when that count is above 50000 call `dm::dm_warn_add` with the text "sheet ", name and " truncated at 50000 rows" and stop. Otherwise build the row text in a scratch list: let tabs be 0 and last be -1; for each cell of the row in order: skip it when its column is not above last (a repeated cell keeps the first one); set last to its column; while tabs is below the column append a tab and add 1 to tabs; append the cell's text from `ol_xp`, with every tab, newline and carriage return of that text replaced by a space. Add the row with `dm::dm_add(dm::DM_ROW, 0, row)`.

`ol_xl_sheet(k int)` reads worksheet number k of `ol_shp`. Let name be its name. The record at its offset must have id 0x0809 and a 16-bit type at data offset 2 of 0x0010 (worksheet), else call `dm::dm_warn_add` with "sheet ", the name and " missing" and return. Add the heading `dm::dm_add(dm::DM_HEAD, 2, name)` and call `dm::dm_new_table()`. Clear the cell lists `ol_cr`, `ol_cc`, `ol_ca`, `ol_cl` and `ol_xp`, set `ol_fpend` to -1, and go through the records after the BOF with depth 0 (stop at the end of the stream or a record that does not fit): id 0x0809 adds 1 to depth; id 0x000A at depth 0 stops, otherwise takes 1 off depth; records at depth above 0 are skipped. At depth 0, with row the 16-bit number at data offset 0, col the one at 2 and ixfe the one at 4, by id: 0x00FD (LABELSST): the 32-bit number at offset 6 is the string number; record the shared string text (`ol_sst` at `ol_ssa` and `ol_ssl`; an empty text when out of range) with `ol_xl_put`; 0x0204 (LABEL) and 0x00D6 (RSTRING): the text is `ol_xl_str` of the data at offset 6 with short false, recorded with `ol_xl_put`; 0x0203 (NUMBER): `ol_xl_numcell` with `binary::read_f64le` of the data at offset 6; 0x027E (RK): `ol_xl_numcell` with `ol_xl_rk` of the 32-bit number at offset 6; 0x00BD (MULRK): the first column is the 16-bit number at offset 2 and the entries start at offset 4, each a 16-bit ixfe and a 32-bit RK, as many as (data length minus 6) divided by 6; entry j is in column first plus j with its own ixfe; 0x0205 (BOOLERR): the byte at offset 6 is the value and the byte at 7 says error when it is 1: record the error name from `ol_xl_err` or "TRUE" for a non-zero boolean and "FALSE" for zero; 0x0006 (FORMULA): the result is the 8 bytes at offset 6; when its last two bytes (offsets 6 and 7 of the result) are both 255 it is a special value by the result's first byte: 0 a string (record an empty cell with `ol_xl_put` and keep its index in `ol_fpend`), 1 a boolean (TRUE when the result's byte 2 is non-zero, else FALSE), 2 an error (`ol_xl_err` of byte 2), 3 an empty string (record an empty cell); otherwise it is a number: `ol_xl_numcell` with `binary::read_f64le` of the data at offset 6; 0x0207 (STRING, the text of the formula before it): when `ol_fpend` is 0 or more, take the text `ol_xl_str` of the data at offset 0 with short false, append it to `ol_xp` and set that cell's position and length to it, then set `ol_fpend` back to -1. After the records call `ol_xl_rows(name)` and `dm::dm_end_table()`.

`ol_xls() bool` reads an Excel workbook from the open compound file: read the stream "Workbook" (or "Book" when that one is missing) into `ol_xb` (false when neither can be read), call `ol_xl_globals()` (false when it is below 0, with `ol_xb` cleared), call `ol_xl_sheet(k)` for every worksheet in order, call `dm::dm_set_meta("sheets", ...)` with the sheet count as decimal text, then `ol_meta(false)`; return true.

## PowerPoint 97 to 2003

The stream "PowerPoint Document" is a tree of records, each with an 8-byte header: a 16-bit field whose low 4 bits are the version and the upper 12 bits the instance, a 16-bit type and a 32-bit data length; records of version 15 are containers whose data is again records. Module state: `var ol_ps byte[>..]` (the stream), `var ol_pid int[>..]` (the stream offset of each persisted object by persist id, -1 when unknown), `var ol_pu int[>..]` (the UTF-16 code units of the text box being read), `var ol_pty = 0` (its text type), `var ol_pskip = false` (set when a field meta character shows that the box is a date, number or footer), `var ol_pph = 0` (the placeholder id of the shape being read), `var ol_pfs = -1` and `var ol_pfe = -1` (the data range of its style atom in `ol_ps`), `var ol_pnotes = false` (text goes to the notes), `var ol_pnt byte[>..]` (the notes text), and for the slides `var ol_sid int[>..]` (slide id), `var ol_spe int[>..]` (persist id), `var ol_sa0 int[>..]` and `var ol_sa1 int[>..]` (the range of the slide's text atoms in the slide list, from its persist atom to the next one), and for the notes `var ol_nsl int[>..]` (the slide id each notes page belongs to), `var ol_npe int[>..]` (its persist id), `var ol_na0 int[>..]` and `var ol_na1 int[>..]`.

`ol_pp_rec(p int, ty int&, ver int&, inst int&, n int&) bool` reads the record header at p of `ol_ps`: ty is the type, ver the low 4 bits of the first 16-bit field, inst its upper 12 bits and n the length; it returns false when the header does not fit or p plus 8 plus n passes the end of `ol_ps`.

`ol_pp_persist(off int) bool` applies the persist directory atom whose record starts at off (type 0x1772, false otherwise): its data is a series of groups, each a 32-bit number whose low 20 bits are the first persist id and upper 12 bits the count c, followed by c 32-bit stream offsets for consecutive ids. Make `ol_pid` long enough (fill with -1 up to the id, ids below 1000000 only) and for each id whose entry is still -1 set it to its offset.

`ol_pp_edits() int` returns the persist id of the document container, or -1 on failure. Read the stream "Current User" (-1 when missing): the 32-bit number at offset 12 is a header token; 0xF3D1C4DF means the file is encrypted: call `dm::dm_warn_add("encrypted PowerPoint document: it needs its password")`, set `ol_enc` true and return -2; any other token than 0xE391C05F returns -1. The 32-bit number at offset 16 is the offset of the newest edit record. Clear `ol_pid`. Follow at most 1000 edit records: at offset e the record must have type 0x0FF5; its data (starting at e plus 8) holds the offset of the previous edit at 8 (32 bits), the offset of its persist directory at 12 and the document persist id at 16; call `ol_pp_persist` on the persist directory offset (stop when it fails); keep the document persist id of the newest edit; stop when the previous offset is 0 or equals e. Return the document persist id.

`ol_pp_pf(p int, n int, total int)` reads the paragraph runs of a style atom whose data is `ol_ps` from p for n bytes, into the module lists `var ol_prs int[>..]` (the first character of each run), `var ol_prv int[>..]` (its indent level) and `var ol_prb int[>..]` (1 for a bullet, 0 for none, -1 for not set): clear them; with c the number of characters covered so far (0) and q the position, repeat while c is not above total and q plus 10 is within the data and fewer than 10000 runs were read: the 32-bit number at q is the character count of the run, the 16-bit number at q plus 4 the indent level, the 32-bit number at q plus 6 the masks; push c, the indent and the bullet state (when bit 0 of the masks is set and the 16-bit number at q plus 10 has bit 0 set then 1, when bit 0 of the masks is set and that bit is clear then 0, else -1), then move q past the fields the masks name, in this order and sizes: 2 bytes of bullet flags when any of the masks 0x0F is set, then 2 bytes each for the masks 0x80, 0x10, 0x40, then 4 bytes for 0x20, then 2 bytes each for 0x800, 0x1000, 0x2000, 0x4000, 0x100, 0x400, 0x8000, then for 0x100000 a tab stop list of a 16-bit count followed by that many times 4 bytes, then 2 bytes for 0x10000, 2 bytes when any of 0xE0000 is set, 2 bytes for 0x200000; add the run's character count to c.

`ol_pp_flush()` emits the text box that was read. Do nothing when `ol_pu` is empty. Otherwise (and in every case at the end) clear `ol_pu`, set `ol_pskip` false and `ol_pfs` and `ol_pfe` to -1. When `ol_pskip` is true, or `ol_pph` is 7, 8, 9 or 10 (date, slide number, footer, header placeholders), emit nothing. Split the units into paragraphs at the unit 0x0D. For every paragraph build UTF-8 text with a scratch UTF-16 byte list and `dm::dm_utf16(units, false, out)` where false means little endian: the units 0x0B become a newline, 0x09 a tab, other units below 32 and 0xFFFF are dropped. When `ol_pnotes` is true append each paragraph that is not blank to `ol_pnt`, separated by a newline from the text before it. Otherwise: when `ol_pty` is 0 or 6 (titles) join all paragraphs with spaces and add `dm::dm_add(dm::DM_HEAD, 2, ...)`; else call `ol_pp_pf` on the style atom (when `ol_pfs` is 0 or more) with the number of units as total, and for each paragraph find the run that holds its first character (the last run whose start is not above it): indent level lvl (0 when there is no run); the paragraph has a bullet when the run's bullet state is 1, or, when it is -1, when `ol_pty` is 1, 5, 7 or 8; it becomes `dm::DM_ITEM` with level lvl plus 1 when lvl is above 0 or it has a bullet, otherwise `dm::DM_PARA` with level 0, added with `dm::dm_add`.

`ol_pp_atoms(p int, end int)` goes through the records of `ol_ps` from p up to end (stopping at a record that does not fit) that make up text boxes, by type: 0x0F9F (text header): call `ol_pp_flush()` first, then `ol_pty` is the 32-bit number at its data; 0x0FA0 (UTF-16 text) and 0x0FA8 (8-bit text): clear `ol_pu` and push the code units, for 0x0FA0 the 16-bit little endian values of the data, for 0x0FA8 one unit equal to each byte; 0x0FA1 (style): set `ol_pfs` and `ol_pfe` to the start and end of its data; 0x0FD8, 0x0FF7, 0x0FF8, 0x0FF9 and 0x0FFA (field meta characters): set `ol_pskip` true. Every other record is skipped. Call `ol_pp_flush()` at the end.

`ol_pp_walk(p int, end int, depth int)` (recursive) goes through the records of `ol_ps` from p to end and handles the drawing of a slide or notes page: a container (version 15) of type 0xF004 (a shape) first sets `ol_pph` to 0, then is walked into; the record 0x0BC3 (placeholder atom) sets `ol_pph` to the byte at offset 4 of its data; a container of type 0xF00D (a text box) calls `ol_pp_atoms` on its data range, with `ol_pph` kept as set by the shape; every other container is walked into with depth plus 1, but only when depth is below 12. Stop at a record that does not fit.

`ol_pp_lists(doc int)` reads the slide lists of the document container at stream offset doc (its type must be 0x03E8). Clear `ol_sid`, `ol_spe`, `ol_sa0`, `ol_sa1`, `ol_nsl`, `ol_npe`, `ol_na0` and `ol_na1`. For each child record of the document of type 0x0FF0 whose instance is 0 (slides) or 2 (notes): go through its child records; a record of type 0x03F3 (a persist atom) starts a new entry: its 32-bit number at data offset 0 is the persist id and the one at 12 the slide id; its range starts at the end of that atom, and the previous entry's range ends at the start of this atom (and the last one's at the end of the list). Slides go to `ol_sid`, `ol_spe`, `ol_sa0` and `ol_sa1`; for notes go to `ol_npe`, `ol_na0` and `ol_na1`, and `ol_nsl` is filled later by `ol_pp_notes_of`.

`ol_pp_notes_of()` fills `ol_nsl`: for each notes entry the notes container is at the stream offset `ol_pid[persist id]` (when valid and of type 0x03F0), its first child must be the record 0x03F1 and the 32-bit number at its data offset 0 is the slide id; push it (or -1 when anything is wrong).

`ol_ppt() bool` reads a PowerPoint file from the open compound file. Read the stream "PowerPoint Document" into `ol_ps` (false when missing). Call `ol_pp_edits()`; when it is -2 return false, when it is -1 call `dm::dm_warn_add("PowerPoint slide directory is damaged")` and return false. The document container is at `ol_pid[that id]`; call `ol_pp_lists` with it and `ol_pp_notes_of()`. Set `dm::dm_set_meta("slides", ...)` to the number of slides as decimal text. For each slide n from 1: call `dm::dm_page(n, "Slide n")` (n as decimal text); set `ol_pnotes` false; call `ol_pp_atoms` on the slide's range from the slide list; then the slide container at `ol_pid[its persist id]`, when it is valid and its type is 0x03EE, goes to `ol_pp_walk` over its data range with depth 0; then look for the notes entry whose slide id equals the slide's id: when found set `ol_pnotes` true, clear `ol_pnt`, call `ol_pp_atoms` on its range in the notes list, then walk the notes container (type 0x03F0) at `ol_pid[its persist id]` with `ol_pp_walk`; set `ol_pnotes` false; when `ol_pnt` is not blank add `dm::dm_add(dm::DM_NOTE, 0, line)` where line is "Notes: " followed by `ol_pnt`. Then `ol_meta(false)`, clear `ol_ps` and return true.

## Outlook messages

A message is a compound file whose top level holds the streams "__substg1.0_XXXXTTTT" (XXXX the property id and TTTT the type as 4 upper-case hex digits each) and the property stream "__properties_version1.0"; every attachment is a storage "__attach_version1.0_#NNNNNNNN" (8 hex digits, counting from 0) with the same kind of streams inside. Type 001F is UTF-16 little endian text, 001E 8-bit text, 0102 binary. Module state: `struct OlAtt` has `nat int`, `nlen int` (the name in `ol_apool`), `tat int`, `tlen int` (the media type in `ol_apool`) and `dat int`, `dlen int` (the data in `ol_apool`); `var ol_atts OlAtt[>..]` and `var ol_apool byte[>..]`.

`ol_att_count() int` is the number of attachments kept. `ol_att_name(i int, out byte[>..]&)`, `ol_att_type(i int, out byte[>..]&)` and `ol_att_data(i int, out byte[>..]&)` clear out and append the name, the media type (lower case, like "image/png") or the data of attachment i; they leave out empty when i is out of range.

`ol_msg_str(prefix const byte[:], id int, out byte[>..]&) bool` appends the text of property id to out and returns true when its stream exists: the name is prefix, then "__substg1.0_", `ol_hex` of id with 4 digits and the type "001F", and the stream is read with `cfb::cf_get` (when it is missing try the type "001E"). A 001F stream goes through `dm::dm_utf16(bytes, false, out)` (false means little endian; a zero unit adds nothing); a 001E stream adds each byte except 0 with `dm::dm_put_cp(out, dm::dm_cp1252(byte))`.

`ol_msg_bin(prefix const byte[:], id int, out byte[>..]&) bool` appends the bytes of the stream prefix, "__substg1.0_", `ol_hex` of id with 4 digits and "0102" to out with `cfb::cf_get`, returning its result.

`ol_msg_time(out byte[>..]&)` appends the message date as text: read the stream "__properties_version1.0"; its entries are 16 bytes each from offset 32: a 16-bit type, a 16-bit property id and then 8 bytes of value at entry offset 8; find the entry of type 0x0040 and id 0x0039, or when there is none the one with id 0x0E06; read its value as the 32-bit low half and the 32-bit high half and call `ol_filetime(high times 4294967296 plus low, out)`.

`ol_msg_who(prefix const byte[:], nameid int, mailid int, mailid2 int, out byte[>..]&)` appends a person: the name (`ol_msg_str` of nameid), the address (`ol_msg_str` of mailid, or when that is empty of mailid2). When both are not empty and differ the person is the name, a space, "<", the address and ">"; otherwise whichever is not empty.

`ol_msg_list(id int, out byte[>..]&)` appends `ol_msg_str("", id, ...)` for a recipient list with every semicolon replaced by a comma and then a space unless one follows, and with spaces after a comma not doubled.

`ol_msg_field(label const byte[:], key const byte[:], text const byte[:])` does nothing when text is blank; otherwise it adds `dm::dm_add(dm::DM_FIELD, 0, ...)` with the label, a colon, a space and the text, and calls `dm::dm_set_meta(key, text)`.

`ol_msg_text(utf8 const byte[:], isHtml bool)` adds a body that is already UTF-8: when isHtml is true call `html::ht_read_utf8`, stopping when it returns true; otherwise (or when it returned false) call `textfmt::tx_text`, and when that returns false add the text as one `dm::DM_PARA` block.

`ol_msg_atts()` reads the attachments. Clear `ol_atts` and `ol_apool`. The attachment count is the 32-bit number at offset 20 of the stream "__properties_version1.0" (at most 10000). For attachment number i, the prefix is "__attach_version1.0_#", `ol_hex(i, 8)` and a slash. Skip it when its data stream (`ol_msg_bin` of 0x3701 into a scratch list) cannot be read. The name is `ol_msg_str` of 0x3707, or when that is empty of 0x3704; the type is `ol_msg_str` of 0x370E lower-cased (ASCII), or "application/octet-stream" when empty. Append name, type and data to `ol_apool` and push an `OlAtt`.

`ol_msg() bool` reads an Outlook message. Clear the attachments. Add the fields in this order: From (`ol_msg_who("", 0x0C1A, 0x5D01, 0x0C1F, ...)`, label "From", key "from"), To (`ol_msg_list(0x0E04, ...)`, "To", "to"), Cc (0x0E03, "Cc", "cc"), Date (`ol_msg_time`, "Date", "date"), Subject (`ol_msg_str("", 0x0037, ...)`, "Subject", "subject"), all through `ol_msg_field`. Then the body: the text of 0x1000 when it is not blank goes to `ol_msg_text(text, false)`; otherwise the binary 0x1013 (or its text form) when not blank goes to `html::ht_read(bytes)` and, when that returns false, to `ol_msg_text(utf8, false)` of the bytes; otherwise when the stream 0x1009 exists call `dm::dm_warn_add("message body is compressed RTF: not decoded")`. Then `ol_msg_atts()`; when `ol_att_count()` is above 0 call `dm::dm_set_meta("attachments", ...)` with the count as decimal text. Return true.

## Entry point

`ol_read(data const byte[:]) bool` opens data with `cfb::cf_open` into `ol_h` (false when it is below 0), sets `ol_enc` to false and clears the attachments, then dispatches on the streams present, tested with `cfb::cf_find` (0 or more means present), in this order: "EncryptedPackage" calls `dm::dm_warn_add("encrypted Office document: it needs its password")`, sets `ol_enc` true and gives false; "WordDocument" calls `ol_doc()`; "Workbook" or "Book" calls `ol_xls()`; "PowerPoint Document" calls `ol_ppt()`; "__properties_version1.0" calls `ol_msg()`. The result is that call's result (false when none is present), after `cfb::cf_close(ol_h)` and setting `ol_h` to -1.
